ong Democratic midterm environment, a split forecaster consensus, and markets tilting to Lindsay James offset that lean. With almost no reliable polling, the race stays a genuine tossup with only a modest Democratic edge.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a shift in the national midterm environment in the final weeks could easily tip this either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 67 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Ashley Hinson (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's 2nd District is an open seat with incumbent Ashley Hinson leaving to run for Senate. The district leans Republican by about four points, but the 2026 national environment favors Democrats, and both parties see a real opportunity. Joe Mitchell has a slight fundraising edge and some outside groups are attacking him, while Lindsay James has kept pace financially and drawn support from Democratic-aligned pollsters.\n\nExpert ratings are divided: some call it Lean R or Tilt R, others Tossup, and a few rate it Lean D for a flip. Prediction markets give the Democrat a narrow edge. With only one usable poll, from July and Democratic-sponsored, showing a one-point race, uncertainty is high. The forecast treats this as a coin-flip-leaning-Democratic contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5595, "margin": 0.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5585, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6246, "margin": 1.5}]}